      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Airway epithelium is a key component for airway integrity. Previously, we found that expression of the Sec14l3 gene that encodes a 45-kDa secretory protein is inversely associated with the progression of experimentally induced airway inflammation and degeneration/necrosis of alveolar epithelium. In this report, using in situ hybridization we demonstrated that the ciliated cells in mouse lung selectively express Sec14l3 mRNA. In a three-dimensional culture of mouse tracheal epithelial cells, levels of the Sec14l3 mRNA correlated with the differentiation of ciliated cells. Intranasal infection of adult mice with influenza virus resulted in a 20-fold, progressive decrease in Sec14l3 mRNA expression over 10 days post infection. These results enhance the potential value of Sec14l3 as a ciliated epithelial cell-specific biomarker for the progression of airway inflammations such as airway viral infection and asthma."},
